<p>A walk through a lively market is full of colour, sound and small surprises, which makes it easy to talk. You can look at crafts, fruit, clothes or books, share a snack, and compare opinions. Keep a hand on your phone and bag in crowded places, and keep your own money for your own purchases.</p>
<p>Food does not have to be fancy. Roasted maize, fresh fruit, chips, samosas or a cup of chai from a busy stall all make good shared moments. A shared snack is also a gentle way to see how someone relaxes. If you like nyama choma, suggest it for a later date at a busy, well-known place in the daytime, rather than a long evening on the first meeting.</p>

<h2>Simple food spots that will not strain your budget</h2>
<p>If you both want a proper meal, you do not need a costly restaurant. Choose a casual, busy eatery in a public area and keep the order simple. Think of a plate of githeri, a light lunch, a plain pizza or a snack and a drink. Agree on the plan beforehand so nobody is surprised by the bill.</p>
