<h2>First date ideas in Nairobi when it rains</h2>
<p>Nairobi weather can change quickly, so it helps to have a backup. Indoor options include a bookshop browse, a gallery visit, a board game cafe, a food court, or a casual chai in a busy mall. Bring an umbrella and message your date early if plans need to shift. A relaxed attitude to a change of plan is itself a good sign.</p>
<p>If you do not live in Nairobi, the same ideas work in other towns. Our guide to <a href="/blog/first-date-ideas-outside-nairobi">first date ideas outside Nairobi</a> covers more places.</p>

<h2>Safety checklist before you go</h2>
<h3>Getting there and getting home</h3>
<p>Plan your travel before the date, not during it. Nairobi traffic can be slow, so leave early and tell your date if you are running late. Use whatever transport you trust, whether that is a matatu, a ride-hailing app or your own car, and always arrange your own way home. Do not rely on the other person to take you home on a first meeting.</p>
<p>Keep your phone charged and carry a little cash for emergencies. If the date ends later than planned, message your friend, share your route, and get home before it is dark whenever you can.</p>
<h3>Your pre-date checklist</h3>
<p>Whatever you choose, keep the basics in place. They take a few minutes and make the whole date more relaxing.</p>
<ol>
<li>Meet in a public place and arrive by your own transport.</li>
<li>Tell a friend or family member who you are meeting, where, and when you will check in.</li>
<li>Share your live location with someone you trust.</li>
<li>Keep your drink in sight and your belongings close.</li>
<li>Do not share your home address or workplace on the first meeting.</li>
<li>Leave if anything feels wrong, without explaining.</li>
</ol>
